Quantum Physics II by Barton Zwiebach MIT 8 05 (@physics-source-quantum: 0, is #reference) { r_0093B354ED191AE21C5AAA51E0
Barton Zwiebach, **MIT 8.05 Quantum Physics II**, Fall 2013. [Official course](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-05-quantum-physics-ii-fall-2013/) · [Lecture-note index](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-05-quantum-physics-ii-fall-2013/pages/lecture-notes/). The current OCW licence link is **CC BY-NC-SA 4.0**; retain attribution, noncommercial/share-alike conditions and separately credited component exceptions. This is a historical course edition. No course media, figure, dataset, solution or source code is bundled.

**Actual inspection — 1 November 2026:** course identity/index and selected actual PDF passages: note 2, dated **17 September 2013**, introductory observation discussion and printed pages **5–9** on analyzer sequences, finite states and operators; its page 6/Figure 5 was visually checked. [Note 2 PDF](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-05-quantum-physics-ii-fall-2013/a56c316e4ec548d8bd0a554fac9a74e8_MIT8_05F13_Chap_02.pdf). The source introduces the multi-analyzer arrangements as thought experiments. The common reconstruction retains **x-minus**, with conditional final z probabilities one-half and original post-preparation input probabilities one-quarter each; do not relabel this as analyzed laboratory data.

Read note 6, dated **4 November 2013**, section 2.1–2.3 passages on printed **10–17**, through constant-H evolution and the commuting-generator condition. [Note 6 PDF](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-05-quantum-physics-ii-fall-2013/79a8091ef4f18d8e3ff76a097e8db33c_MIT8_05F13_Chap_06.pdf). The common block supplies its own already proved finite matrix-series justification. Selected note 8 passages, dated **14 December 2013**, printed **1–3 and 8–10**, introduce distinguishable tensor factors, factorization and the Φ₀ Bell state. [Note 8 PDF](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-05-quantum-physics-ii-fall-2013/ffe665c0cba2eae19a83e88dec42925c_MIT8_05F13_Chap_08.pdf). No complete-course or laboratory-reproduction claim follows from these inspections.

**Progressive reading:** work note 2 in full alongside the new finite state/measurement lessons; use notes 3–4 to deepen and translate the shared linear algebra. Read note 5 for uncertainty and compatible observables, note 6 sections 2.1–2.3 for dynamics, and note 8 sections 1–3 for composite states. The rest of the oscillator, two-state applications, angular momentum, multiparticle and advanced course forms branch continuation. Complete selected problems independently; whole notes not named in the inspection above, videos, assignments and exams are reading/work expectations, not newly inspected or completed results.

Compare the complementary [Quantum Mechanics — David Tong](../../engineering/materials-engineering/sources/Quantum-Mechanics-Tong.lingua#r_0GY6BHCRA9CR5YGBY1AT9DRZY0) wavefunction account, keeping source assumptions and approximations explicit. The common density, Bloch, loss and independent-check programs are original finite derivations; they are not copied course implementations. Physical postulates, ideal instruments, mathematical consequences and actual observations remain distinct.

**Return:** [Physics](../Physics.lingua#r_04A5CP8RTBERZEN4XCCDQ3T47W).
